3
00:00:09.263 --> 00:00:17.091
(narrator) It opens on a manila case file, number 001: Every module should keep a secret, a song about information hiding, stamped classified.

4
00:00:17.091 --> 00:00:22.111
(narrator) The intro: a huge Y2K, then a red stamp, the most famous bug, as a beetle crawls in.

5
00:00:22.111 --> 00:00:30.058
(narrator) A vintage 1960s mainframe has two large tape reels and a small enlarged cutaway of its core memory: rings threaded on crossing wires.

6
00:00:30.058 --> 00:00:33.644
(narrator) Memory was expensive: dollar bills fly into its core memory.

7
00:00:33.644 --> 00:00:40.755
(narrator) Split-flap digits for 1985 lose their first two digits, only 85 is stored, and a few bills drop back out of the memory.

8
00:00:40.755 --> 00:00:51.750
(narrator) A two-digit field rolls from 99 to 00, and a green-phosphor terminal shows the computer reading 19 plus 00 as 1900, and a desk calendar tears from December 31, 1999 to January 1, 1900.

9
00:00:51.750 --> 00:01:04.776
(narrator) Another terminal file computes an age as 00 minus 85, which is minus 85 years old, while a young man born in 85 rewinds into a teen, a kid, a baby, a newborn, an egg cell, and then a puff of smoke with a question mark.

10
00:01:04.776 --> 00:01:14.516
(narrator) Twelve case files, from payroll to grades, all carry the same red YY secret tag and turn red; an intercept console searches for YY and finds it in file after file.

11
00:01:14.516 --> 00:01:27.902
(narrator) A mission clock turns over to January 1, 2000, celebratory fireworks bloom around it, almost nothing breaks, and red dollar bills fly away from behind a classified expense sheet that totals billions of dollars, stamped paid.

12
00:01:27.902 --> 00:01:34.893
(narrator) A case file gives the title, Every module should keep a secret, stamped top secret, while your hero sits at a laptop.

13
00:01:34.893 --> 00:01:46.605
(narrator) Then the title shrinks into a corner and, as your hero watches, a spy caper plays: two module agents, case files in fedoras and sunglasses, tiptoe in and hide behind newspapers from a searchlight.

14
00:01:46.605 --> 00:01:57.122
(narrator) Agent 007, the Payment Gateway, drops in upside down on a rope, backflips down, and builds a laser barrier between them and the rest of the system, then stands guard beside it.

15
00:01:57.122 --> 00:02:02.022
(narrator) A nosy third agent tries to peek, gets zapped, and asks the gateway agent instead.

16
00:02:02.022 --> 00:02:07.101
(narrator) Everyone says shh, the agent raises his martini, and the scene is stamped classified.

17
00:02:07.101 --> 00:02:12.033
[music]

18
00:02:12.033 --> 00:02:15.253
Y2K. Maybe the most famous bug.

19
00:02:15.593 --> 00:02:19.053
Back in the sixties, memory was expensive.

20
00:02:19.313 --> 00:02:21.513
So programmers saved space:

21
00:02:21.513 --> 00:02:24.733
they stored the year with just two digits.

22
00:02:25.513 --> 00:02:27.893
1985? Became “85.”

23
00:02:28.173 --> 00:02:31.653
Then comes the year 2000: “double-zero.”

24
00:02:31.733 --> 00:02:34.653
And the computer reads it as… 1900.

25
00:02:34.873 --> 00:02:38.193
Born in ’85? Zero minus 85:

26
00:02:38.393 --> 00:02:40.693
you’re negative 85 years old.

27
00:02:40.693 --> 00:02:43.973
Nobody thought this code would still run in 2000.

28
00:02:44.053 --> 00:02:45.153
It did.

29
00:02:45.293 --> 00:02:48.333
And the two-digit year wasn’t hidden in one module.

30
00:02:48.333 --> 00:02:51.013
It was copied into every calculation.

31
00:02:51.173 --> 00:02:53.553
So engineers spent years hunting down

32
00:02:53.553 --> 00:02:55.873
every line that touched a year.

33
00:02:55.873 --> 00:02:58.913
Midnight came… and almost nothing broke,

34
00:02:58.913 --> 00:03:01.153
because they fixed it in time.

35
00:03:01.413 --> 00:03:03.853
The bill? Billions.

36
00:03:04.173 --> 00:03:08.913
Lesson one: every module should keep a secret.

37
00:03:08.913 --> 00:03:20.600
[music]

38
00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:21.981
(narrator) Verse 1, why it matters.

39
00:03:21.981 --> 00:03:31.245
(narrator) A vector portrait recreates Margaret Hamilton’s 1969 pose beside the bound Apollo listings: long hair, round glasses, patterned dress, and one hand on the stack.

40
00:03:31.245 --> 00:03:36.596
(narrator) Her record credits her as lead software engineer for Apollo’s onboard flight software at MIT.

41
00:03:36.596 --> 00:03:43.616
(narrator) Apollo 11, 1969: about 80 thousand lines of code across the command and lunar modules, excluding comments and blank lines.

42
00:03:43.616 --> 00:03:48.162
(narrator) Modern car, 2021: about 100 million lines across the vehicle, a Bosch estimate.

43
00:03:48.162 --> 00:03:51.845
(narrator) The counts use different scopes, not a precise complexity ratio.

44
00:03:51.845 --> 00:03:58.577
(narrator) A head opens like a hinged lid; three case files fit, then more cram in and papers burst out: no one can hold it all.

45
00:03:58.577 --> 00:04:05.309
(narrator) On a detective’s corkboard, red string joins every pair of four pinned modules, 6 connections; eight modules make 28.

46
00:04:05.309 --> 00:04:16.530
(narrator) When everything depends on everything, the strings pull taut, the cards tremble, and the whole board collapses into one Big Ball of Mud that lands with a splatter and stays beside the next scene.

47
00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:22.284
(narrator) One changed file gets eleven more stamped hit, and stamping out one bug brings two more crawling in.

48
00:04:22.873 --> 00:04:24.873
♪ Apollo’s code, printed out, ♪

49
00:04:24.873 --> 00:04:27.953
♪ stood as tall as Margaret Hamilton. ♪

50
00:04:28.453 --> 00:04:30.893
♪ A modern car? A hundred million lines. ♪

51
00:04:30.893 --> 00:04:33.913
♪ We’re not a thousand times smarter than back then, ♪

52
00:04:33.913 --> 00:04:36.733
♪ so no one holds the whole thing in their head. ♪

53
00:04:36.733 --> 00:04:39.933
♪ Four modules? Six connections. ♪

54
00:04:39.933 --> 00:04:42.373
♪ Eight modules? Twenty-eight. ♪

55
00:04:42.513 --> 00:04:45.073
♪ Let every part depend on every other part, ♪

56
00:04:45.073 --> 00:04:47.613
♪ and you’ve built a Big Ball of Mud: ♪

57
00:04:47.613 --> 00:04:50.613
♪ change one thing, touch dozens of files; ♪

58
00:04:50.613 --> 00:04:55.153
♪ fix one bug, and two more appear. ♪

59
00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:58.658
(narrator) Pre-chorus: how to split a system.

60
00:04:58.658 --> 00:05:05.069
(narrator) A flowchart of steps rides a conveyor belt, read, process, print, and gets a red stamp: not by steps.

61
00:05:05.069 --> 00:05:13.067
(narrator) Then, in a searchlight, the title page of Parnas’s 1972 paper, On the Criteria To Be Used in Decomposing Systems into Modules.

62
00:05:13.673 --> 00:05:16.793
♪ So how do you split a system into modules? ♪

63
00:05:16.793 --> 00:05:19.953
